# 1. Core Theory (Days 1-2)

## 1.1 The Two-Mode Market Model

Ehlers' foundational premise: markets operate in exactly two modes — trend mode and cycle mode. Trend-following indicators (MAMA/FAMA) work in trend mode but generate false signals in cycle mode. Oscillators work in cycle mode but generate false signals in trend mode. The Cyber Cycle targets the cycle component of price — the oscillation around the trend that trend-followers ignore.

## 1.2 What the Cyber Cycle Actually Is

The Cyber Cycle is a 2nd-order IIR (Infinite Impulse Response) digital resonant filter. It is not a ratio, percentage, or comparison — it is a bandpass filter tuned to resonate at the dominant cycle frequency. The three terms of the formula serve distinct DSP roles:

| **Term** | **Role** | **Effect** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| gain * (Filt[0] - 2*Filt[1] + Filt[2]) | Second difference of input | Discrete 2nd derivative — kills trend, emphasizes cycle |
| + 2*pole * CycPrev1 | Resonance (spring term) | Positive feedback from 1 bar ago — creates persistent oscillation |
| - pole*pole * CycPrev2 | Damping (stability term) | Negative feedback from 2 bars ago — prevents runaway |

## 1.3 Parameter Semantics

**alpha=0.07** is the single free parameter. It controls:
- pole radius = 1 - alpha = 0.93 (close to unit circle = light damping = persistent oscillation)
- Time constant ~1/alpha ~14 bars (targets 14-28 bar dominant cycle range typical of EUR/USD D1)
- Output amplitude (strong effect — 47% variation across 0.04-0.20 range)
- Signal frequency (weak effect — only 14% variation across same range)

## 1.4 The Trigger Line

Trigger = 2*Cycle[1] - Cycle[3] is a linear extrapolation — it estimates where the Cycle would be if it continued at its current rate. This makes it lead the Cycle by ~1-2 bars. Trigger crossing above Cycle = long signal. Trigger crossing below = short signal.

## 1.5 Why RoofingFilter as Input

Raw price contains trend (low frequency) + dominant cycle + noise (high frequency). Feeding raw price into any oscillator: trend causes drift/saturation, noise gets amplified by the second-difference term. RoofingFilter removes both — its output contains primarily the dominant cycle band. CyberCycle applied to RoofingFilter output operates on a pre-cleaned signal.

# 2. Implementation Notes (Day 3)

## 2.1 Zorro-Specific Issues Discovered This Week

| **Issue** | **Wrong** | **Correct** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Trig function units | sin/cos take degrees (assumed) | sin/cos take RADIANS — standard C math library |
| series() overwrite pattern | Works reliably for IIR feedback | Unreliable in included files — use static vars instead |
| Variable namespace | Locals are function-scoped | Flat namespace — all included files share same space |
| RoofingFilter call | Called inside CyberCycle | Must be called once in test script, result passed as series |
| Script structure | LookBack=200 + manual barCount | Use void run() + if(is(LOOKBACK)) return; pattern |

## 2.2 Flat Namespace Collision

SuperSmoother2Pole.c uses local variables: c1, c2, c3, c0, b1, Filt. CyberCycle's initial implementation used the same names. All locals collide in Zorro's shared namespace even when neither function is being called at the same time. Fix: prefix all CyberCycle locals with cc_ (cc_pole, cc_gain, cc_Input, cc_alpha, cc_out).

## 2.3 Static Variables for IIR State

The series()-then-overwrite pattern was attempted for IIR feedback state. Results were incorrect. The reliable pattern for self-referential IIR in included files is static variables:

    static var CycPrev1 = 0;
    static var CycPrev2 = 0;
    Cyc = formula using CycPrev1, CycPrev2;
    CycPrev2 = CycPrev1;
    CycPrev1 = Cyc;

## 2.4 Double-Call Bug

CyberCycle originally called RoofingFilter internally. When the test script also called it for CSV export, RoofingFilter ran twice per bar — double-shifting its internal series and corrupting state. This was the first symptom leading to the trig bug discovery. Fix: CyberCycle takes vars cc_Input. Test script calls RoofingFilter once and passes the result.

# 3. Critical Discovery: Trig Functions Use Radians

## 3.1 Root Cause

During Day 3 debugging, alpha_debug = -2.29027 instead of expected 0.0885. With alpha1 = -2.29, pole radius = 3.29 — outside unit circle, guaranteed unstable. Diagnosis: 0.707*360.0/48.0 = 5.3025. With degree-based trig: cos(5.3025 degrees) = 0.9957 → alpha1 = 0.0885 (correct). With radians: cos(5.3025 rad) ≈ 0.557 → alpha1 ≈ -2.29 (observed). Confirmed by Zorro documentation: sin/cos/tan take angle in radians.

## 3.2 Why Test_RoofingFilter.c Was Unaffected

Test_RoofingFilter.c called HighPassFilter twice per bar (once directly, once inside RoofingFilter). The double-shift accidentally created a different effective computation that produced plausible-looking output. Single-call scripts exposed the explosion. Bug was present since Week 5 and undetected until Week 8.

## 3.3 Audit and Fixes

| **File** | **Action** | **Change** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| HighPassFilter.c | FIXED | 0.707*360/N → 0.707*2*PI/N |
| SuperSmoother2Pole.c | FIXED | 180/N → PI/N |
| SuperSmoother3Pole.c | FIXED | 180/N → PI/N |
| UltimateSmoother.c | FIXED | 180/N → PI/N |
| DominantCycle.c | OK | 1.414*180/N*PI/180 — 180s cancel |
| InstantTrendline.c | OK | Explicit *PI/180 conversion |
| MAMA.c | OK | atan()*180/PI — deliberate conversion |
| RoofingFilter.c | OK | No direct trig |
| Decycler.c | OK | No direct trig |
| CyberCycle.c | OK | No trig functions |

All four fixed indicators re-validated: HighPassFilter ✅ SuperSmoother ✅ RoofingFilter ✅ Decycler ✅

Standing rule added: Zorro Light-C sin/cos/tan take RADIANS (standard C). All Ehlers formulas must use radians.

