| 0.15 | 0.004453 | 560 | 3.99 |
| 0.20 | 0.003746 | 580 | 3.86 |

**Decision: alpha=0.07 retained (Ehlers default).**

Alpha controls amplitude strongly (47% variation). Weak effect on signal frequency (14% variation). Default 0.07 is on the steeper part of the amplitude decay curve — optimal balance. For BTC, fix AGC scale first; alpha tuning is secondary.

# 6. NNFX Component Mapping

| **Indicator** | **NNFX Role** | **Notes** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CyberCycle | C1/C2 confirmation | Cycle regime only — must be gated by MAMA/FAMA trend filter |

NNFX use pattern: MAMA above FAMA (bullish) + narrow MAMA/FAMA spread (cycle regime) + Trigger crosses above Cycle = long entry. Wide spread = strong trend = suppress Cyber Cycle signals.

Zero-line crossings (Cycle crosses 0) may reduce micro-crossover noise vs Trigger/Cycle crossovers. Worth testing in strategy development phase.

# 7. Key Findings & Insights

## 7.1 Technical Findings

- Cyber Cycle amplitude is scale-dependent without AGC. EUR/USD D1: ±0.006 std. BTC H4: ±654 std. 100,000x difference — purely from dollar volatility. AGC normalization mandatory for cross-asset use or BTC strategy use.

- alpha controls amplitude (47% variation) far more than signal frequency (14% variation). When choosing alpha for a specific application, target amplitude first.

- The series()-then-overwrite IIR pattern that works in standalone indicators is unreliable in included files. Static variables are the correct pattern for IIR state in Zorro included indicators.

- Zorro's flat variable namespace requires unique prefix per indicator. Common names (c1, c2, Filt, alpha) conflict silently across included files.

## 7.2 Cross-Asset Findings

- Signal frequency is consistent across assets: EUR/USD D1 at 4.3 bars/signal, BTC H4 at 4.5 bars/signal. The oscillator cycles at similar rates regardless of underlying asset.

- EUR/USD amplitude is stable across the full 9-year test window including 2022 downtrend. BTC amplitude varies by 10x between quiet (early 2020) and bull market (2021).

- Both assets: cycle mean is essentially zero — the oscillator is correctly centered regardless of absolute price level.

## 7.3 Conceptual Insights

- A 14% difference in signal rate between trend mode and cycle mode (4.9 vs 4.3) confirms Ehlers' two-mode model empirically: an oscillator cannot detect its own irrelevance in trend mode without external regime information.

- The double-call bug (incorrect implementation producing plausible output) demonstrates that visual inspection is insufficient for validation. Intermediate values must be computed and verified explicitly.

- The formula has exactly one free parameter (alpha). Minimal degrees of freedom = minimal overfitting risk = maximum robustness.

